Amtrak's CEO blames a freight train  operator for its latest deadly accident  an engineer and conductor were killed in  the crash in South Carolina yesterday a  hundred and sixteen others were hurt  Amtrak silverstar going from New York to  Miami was carrying nearly 150 people  when it rammed into a freight train Kris  Van Cleave is in West Columbia South  Carolina near the crash Kris good  morning good morning this is the third  deadly Amtrak crash in less than two  months the train came down these tracks  after encountering a switch that was  apparently in the wrong position that  put the Train on a deadly collision  course drone footage after the collision  shows the damage derailed passenger cars  lay next to a mangled freight engine  Amtrak train 91 had just left Columbia  South Carolina at around 2:35 Sunday  morning investigators say a track switch  was locked in the wrong position  diverting the Amtrak train onto a side  track where it collided with the parked  freight train and derailed the only  thing you heard was just the train car  just shaking shaking shaking the seats  came up off the floor  the National Transportation Safety Board  is now investigating why the switch was  incorrectly set about 80% of freight  railroads across the country do not have  automatic stop technology called  positive train control or PTC installed  NTSB Chairman Roberts some walls a fully  operational positive train control  system could have avoided this accident  that's what it's designed to do  initial reports are the train was going  about 59 miles an hour when it struck  the CSX freight train take a look at  what that does to the passenger cars  they are off the tracks investigators  are now moving car by car inside there  as they are trying to piece together  what happened here and clearly it was a  violent collision killed in the crash  was conductor Michael Sela  and engineer Michael Kemp Kemp's brother  Richard said his brother's death could  have been prevented well somebody forgot  to throw a switch we're all human we all  make mistakes  so and it happened he forgot to throw  that switch  freight line CSX controls these tracks  the company in a statement expressed its  condolences and says it is working with  investigators but did not address why  that switch was in the wrong position  the NTSB has recovered the  forward-facing video camera on that  Amtrak train Gail a lot of questions  about the switch very hard to hear thank  you very much Chris
 

